---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import DecisionTimeline from "../../../components/story/DecisionTimeline.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import StoryState from "../../../components/StoryState.astro";
import StateEvent from "../../../components/StateEvent.astro";
import { publishedState, eventPath } from "../../../lib/state.mjs";
import { claim } from "../../../lib/claims.mjs";
import { event, computed, timeline, sideBySide } from "../../../data/fbi-officials-charged-2026-10-20-two-ex-fbi-officials-charged-with-lying-to-congress.mjs";
const story = "fbi-officials-charged--2026-10-20-two-ex-fbi-officials-charged-with-lying-to-congress";
const short = (id) => claim(story, id).short;
const timelineEntries = timeline.map((t) => ({ date: t.date, title: t.title, current: t.current, cites: [{ c: t.c }] }));
const state = publishedState().events.get("event-fbi-officials-charged-2026-10-20-two-ex-fbi-officials-charged-with-lying-to-congress");
if (!state) throw new Error("Missing publication selection: event-fbi-officials-charged-2026-10-20-two-ex-fbi-officials-charged-with-lying-to-congress");
if (state.status === "merged") return Astro.redirect(eventPath(state.survivor), 301);
---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/fbi-officials-charged/">FBI officials charged</a><span>October 20, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">FBI officials charged &middot; October 20, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updated}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}<Cite c="floris-role" /><Cite c="benavides-role" /><Cite c="surrendered-oct-23" /><Cite c="prelim-oct-27" /></p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#side-by-side">What they said, and what prosecutors say was true</a></li>
          <li><a href="#why-now">Why now, and why this way</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-sides">The two sides</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The Catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What happens next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Federal prosecutors in Washington charged two former FBI officials, Nikki Floris, 46, of Alexandria, Virginia, and Bradley Benavides, 56, of Aiken, South Carolina, with one count each of making false statements to Congress.<Cite c="ages-homes" /><Cite c="charged-one-count" /> The complaint was filed on October 20 under seal, kept from the public, and on October 23 both surrendered, the case was made public, and both were released after a first court appearance.<Cite c="sealed-oct-20" /><Cite c="surrendered-oct-23" /><Cite c="released-oct-23" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In 2020 the two led the FBI's Foreign Influence Task Force, the bureau's unit for protecting that year's election from foreign interference: Floris headed it as a deputy assistant director, and Benavides was its section chief.<Cite c="floris-role" /><Cite c="benavides-role" /><Cite c="fitf-mission" /> Prosecutors say both lied to House Judiciary Committee staff in 2023 about the FBI's investigations of Hunter Biden: what they knew of them and their part in them.<Cite c="forum" /><Cite c="charge-scope" /> By late 2019 the bureau's Baltimore office was investigating him for tax and money-laundering offenses.<Cite c="hb-investigations" /> Floris, prosecutors say, falsely denied any part in those investigations and said she knew of no task force discussion of him.<Cite c="floris-said-no-involvement" /><Cite c="floris-said-no-discussion" /><Cite c="floris-mostly-not-laptop" /> Benavides, they say, falsely claimed he knew the FBI had Hunter Biden's laptop only from a Wikipedia article, not from his FBI work.<Cite c="benavides-said-wikipedia" /><Cite c="benavides-mostly-laptop" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The charges are allegations, and both are presumed innocent.<Cite c="presumed-innocent" /> No grand jury has voted on the case yet. A judge has set a hearing for October 27, where prosecutors must show there is probable cause for the charges, unless a grand jury indicts the two first.<Cite c="complaint-not-indictment" /><Cite c="prelim-oct-27" /><Cite c="prelim-unless-indicted" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="side-by-side">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What they said, and what prosecutors say was true</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Each statement was made in a transcribed interview with committee staff in the Rayburn House Office Building, taken down by a court reporter after a warning that knowingly false answers could be prosecuted.<Cite c="forum" /> No oath is needed for the false-statements law, which reaches any investigation run under a congressional committee's authority.<Cite c="1001-reaches-committees" /> By then Floris had become the intelligence chief of the FBI's Washington field office, and Benavides had left the bureau.<Cite c="later-titles" /><Cite c="benavides-left-2022" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The complaint comes with a sworn statement that sets their answers against documents and messages from the time and against what two FBI employees told investigators this month. In the tables below, each answer is quoted or summarized from that statement, and the government's account beside it has not been tested in court.<Cite c="charge-scope" /><Cite c="witnesses-oct-2026" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Floris, interviewed July 19, 2023</strong></figcaption>
          <div class="data-table-wrap">
            <table class="data-table">
              <thead><tr><th>What she told the committee</th><th>What prosecutors say was true</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                {sideBySide.floris.map(([said, truth]) => (
                  <tr><td>{short(said)}<Cite c={said} /></td><td><em>Alleged:</em> {short(truth)}<Cite c={truth} /></td></tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </figure>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Benavides, interviewed Sept. 28, 2023</strong></figcaption>
          <div class="data-table-wrap">
            <table class="data-table">
              <thead><tr><th>What he told the committee</th><th>What prosecutors say was true</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                {sideBySide.benavides.map(([said, truth]) => (
                  <tr><td>{short(said)}<Cite c={said} /></td><td><em>Alleged:</em> {short(truth)}<Cite c={truth} /></td></tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Two answers carried qualifiers. Floris said there was no discussion of Hunter Biden at the task force "At least not with me," and Benavides, while saying he knew the New York Post's laptop story only from his interview preparation, said he had been aware in 2020 of "a Hunter Biden laptop narrative" in public.<Cite c="floris-said-no-discussion" /><Cite c="benavides-said-prep" /> The law reaches a false answer only if it is material and made knowingly and willfully.<Cite c="1001-materially-false" /> To show the answers mattered, the sworn statement says a committee staffer who sat in on both interviews told the FBI the answers were materially untrue and kept the committee from follow-up interviews and subpoenas, and it says the two answered as they did to hide the task force's role.<Cite c="staffer-materiality" /><Cite c="purpose-conceal" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="why-now">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Why now, and why this way</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The interviews took place in 2023, more than three years before the charges, and no deadline forced the timing: the five-year limit on the charge would have allowed it until {computed.limitYear}.<Cite c="forum" /><Cite c="no-deadline" /> The two FBI employees whose accounts the sworn statement relies on, a Criminal Division unit chief and a Baltimore supervisor on the Hunter Biden case, were interviewed this month.<Cite c="witnesses-oct-2026" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Prosecutors say the charges stem from an ongoing investigation of the task force, which was disbanded in 2025.<Cite c="ongoing-fitf-investigation" /><Cite c="fitf-mission" /> On August 12, two months before the charges, FBI Director Kash Patel said the bureau was investigating the task force's use of Round River.<Cite c="fbi-review-round-river" /><Cite c="sealed-oct-20" /> That was a project the task force's analysts began in 2019. By the account of the national intelligence director's office, it gathered unflattering informant reports about Hunter Biden and others in order to flag them as Russian disinformation. Floris and Benavides were among the officials who approved its first report.<Cite c="round-river-what" /> The House Judiciary Committee demanded Round River records in September.<Cite c="judiciary-pressure" /> Patel also said Floris was on record claiming to be "running a shadow government"; she wrote "I'm basically running a shadow government across the FBI at this point" in a September 2020 message about a recalled intelligence report on China, made public in July.<Cite c="shadow-government-origin" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>From the laptop to the charges</strong></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={timelineEntries} heading="What happened" />
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Prosecutors brought the case by complaint, which lets them charge before a grand jury votes. The Associated Press noted that prosecutors more often use complaints when they need to take someone into custody urgently.<Cite c="complaint-not-indictment" /> In court on October 23, Magistrate Judge Zia Faruqui said the way the case was charged seemed strange and that it is "rare that we have charges this old coming in this nature," and, the Washington Post reported, questioned whether the alleged false statements had a material effect on the congressional investigation.<Cite c="judge-materiality" /> When the prosecutor asked for two weeks or more before the probable-cause hearing because he and the case agent were from out of town, the judge refused: "You have charged this case," he said. "So you will be spending a lot of time in town."<Cite c="judge-strange" /><Cite c="prosecutor-out-of-town" /> Benavides's lawyer had asked for the hearing "as soon as humanly possible." The judge ordered both sides back at 11 a.m. Tuesday. "This is the Sword of Damocles hanging over their heads," he said. "This will follow them for the rest of their lives."<Cite c="judge-damocles" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The case was made public {computed.daysUnsealedToElection} days before the November 3 midterm elections.<Cite c="unsealed-11-days" /> Justice Department rules forbid prosecutors to time charges in order to affect an election and require them to consult the department's Public Integrity Section when a step could look that way. The rules set no quiet period before an election.<Cite c="election-rule" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-sides">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The two sides</h2>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The government</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Prosecutors say the two knowingly lied to keep Congress from learning how closely the task force worked with the Hunter Biden investigators.<Cite c="purpose-conceal" /> U.S. Attorney Jeanine Pirro said that when senior FBI agents lie to Congress "believing they are above the law, it's time to bring the hammer of justice down on them."<Cite c="pirro-hammer" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The defendants</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Floris's lawyers say she was accused "on the say-so of politicians, not on the facts," and that "It's no coincidence the government is filing these nonsense charges now, years later, on the eve of an election." Benavides's lawyers call the case "baseless criminal charges against him in a transparently political complaint," and his lawyer Ron Safer told the court the government "has had the information about this for years."<Cite c="floris-lawyers" /><Cite c="benavides-lawyers" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">What the filing says about the task force's role</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The government's own filing describes what the task force did in the Hunter Biden cases: oversight, keeping information straight between FBI offices and briefing bureau leaders.<Cite c="hb-investigations" /> When officials including Floris and Benavides briefed the case team in August 2020, the filing says, the investigators raised no objection to the task force's proposed briefings and asked the task force to run intelligence checks on Hunter Biden and his companies.<Cite c="case-team-asked" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">In Congress</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Sen. Chuck Grassley, whom the two briefed in 2020, said the charges show "accountability efforts in action"; Rep. Jim Himes, the top Democrat on the House Intelligence Committee, called them an attack on career intelligence officials.<Cite c="grassley-reaction" /><Cite c="himes-reaction" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The Catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>Not a laptop case alone</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">CBS reported the charges as false statements about the investigation involving Hunter Biden's laptop, Fox's page title said the two were charged for lying about the laptop, and Al Jazeera wrote that they had told Congress the unit was not involved in the laptop investigation.<Cite c="headlines-laptop" /> That fits Benavides, whose answers that prosecutors call false are mostly about the laptop. It does not fit the case as a whole: the charge is about their part in the FBI's Hunter Biden investigations, and of the five subjects of Floris's answers the government quotes as false, only one is the laptop.<Cite c="benavides-mostly-laptop" /><Cite c="charge-scope" /><Cite c="floris-mostly-not-laptop" /></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Smaller errors</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">An early version of the Associated Press story said the two were charged over their roles in investigating where the laptop came from; no answer the government charges as false is about that.<Cite c="ap-origins" /><Cite c="no-origins-charge" /></p>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The U.S. attorney's announcement says Benavides claimed he learned of the laptop from Wikipedia. In his quoted testimony he credits Wikipedia for knowing the FBI had it, and says he knew of the laptop story in 2020; he named Wikipedia for the criminal investigation too, then said he did not recall that.<Cite c="release-wikipedia-overstated" /></p>
              <p data-layer="narrative">A New York Post editorial called the charges perjury and said the two had discussions that involved writing the laptop off as Russian disinformation. The charge is false statements, and the lines Benavides added to the FBI director's talking points in October 2020, with Floris's approval, said the FBI had not determined whether the laptop story was Russian disinformation, by the sworn statement's account.<Cite c="nypost-disinfo-claim" /><Cite c="charged-one-count" /><Cite c="talking-points-not-determined" /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">At 11 a.m. on Tuesday, October 27, prosecutors are due to lay out their evidence of probable cause before a judge; the hearing is dropped if a grand jury indicts the two first.<Cite c="prelim-oct-27" /><Cite c="prelim-unless-indicted" /> Federal law gives prosecutors {computed.indictmentDays} days from the arrest, with some exceptions, to file an indictment or information.<Cite c="indictment-30-days" /> Neither step decides whether the answers were knowingly false; that would take a trial. The charge carries a maximum of five years in prison.<Cite c="max-five-years" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      <StoryState view={state} supporting />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>}
